      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:c11a069d82234528",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did bharttāvŭ mean, and what did bhārya mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: പഴം",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "പഴം (paḻaṁ) — \"a fruit\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Behind the fruit sits a verb of ripening. Proto-Dravidian paḻ, 'to ripen, to grow old', handed this family both a fruit and an age, and Malayalam kept the pair close enough that a learner can still hear it: പഴയ (paḻaya), the \"old\" from the run of adjectives, is that same root wearing an adjective ending."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tamil says பழம் (paḻam), which is very nearly the identical word — the ordinary state of affairs between these two closest sisters. Telugu went to పండు (paṇḍu) and Kannada to ಹಣ್ಣು (haṇṇu), and the Kannada form has lost its opening p- to a shift Malayalam sat out entirely."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ഴ in the middle is the letter this book has already called Malayalam's own oddity, the one only these two sisters still write."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five things you might ask someone for."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "paḻaṁ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *paḻaṁ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "it once more, slowly",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: it once more, slowly]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "paḻaṁ, then dayavāyi paḻaṁ tarū — \"please give a fruit\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *paḻaṁ*, then *dayavāyi paḻaṁ tarū* — \"please give a fruit\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does പഴം (paḻaṁ) mean? (\"a fruit\".) Say it once more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
