        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: വിളക്ക്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "വിളക്ക് (viḷakkŭ) — \"a lamp\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A native word, built on a root about shining. Tamil விளக்கு (viḷakku) is the same word, and in both languages a related sense — making something clear, shining a light on it — sits alongside the object."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The object itself has a particular shape in Kerala: the നിലവിളക്ക് (nilaviḷakkŭ), the tall standing brass lamp lit at a doorway on an occasion. Its first piece is നില (nila), the ground it stands on."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada reached for a borrowed word here, ದೀಪ (dīpa) from Sanskrit, where Malayalam kept its own. Malayalam has ദീപം (dīpaṁ) as well, in temple and festival senses, which leaves the everyday lamp to the older resident."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three. A fruit, a cloth, a lamp."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
