        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ഉപ്പ്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ഉപ്പ് (uppŭ) — \"salt\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Proto-Dravidian uppu, and the whole family kept it very nearly untouched: Tamil உப்பு, Telugu ఉప్పు, Kannada ಉಪ್ಪು. Four sisters, one word, four spellings of it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "There is a p sitting inside this word, and in Kannada it did not turn into an h the way the p of its fruit-word did. The shift that separated ಹಣ್ಣು from പഴം (paḻaṁ) reached only the FIRST sound of a word. Inside one, the p stays where it was put — which is why salt looks so alike on all four sides of the family."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The final ് here is the half-vowel this book romanizes -ŭ: a consonant let go of rather than cut off."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four. Salt, and the fruit, and the cloth, and the lamp."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
